  "_exec_summary": [
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 0,
      "FacilityID": 1000122008,
      "SummaryText": "ACCIDENTAL RELEASE PREVENTION AND EMERGENCY RESPONSE POLICIES\nAmbit Chemical Technologies receives, stores and handles more than 100 different chemicals for manufacturing chemical products. The safety and health of employees is our number one priority at the plant, followed closely by the health and safety of our neighbors and the protection of the environment.\n\nTo minimize any risk to people and the environment, Ambit follows state and federal guidelines in the storage and handling of these chemicals. Spill prevention practices include plant inspections every working shift, securing valves on storage tanks and totes, and continual visual monitoring during the transfer of all chemicals. The company also maintains written emergency response plans in the event of a spill or accidental release. The plan includes procedures for notification to the local fire authority and emergency response agencies, and notification to any potentially affected neighbors.\n\nTHE FACILITY AND REGULATED SUBSTANCES HANDLED\nAmbit Chemical Technologies is located in the Bernard Bayou Industrial Park in Gulfport, which is in Harrison County, Mississippi. The company is located on an 11-acre parcel of land stretching between Seaway Road and Bernard Bayou.\nAmbit Chemical handles only two chemicals that are considered hazardous in quantities above threshold limits according to the List of Regulated Toxic Substances of 40CFR part 68.130. The chemicals are Formaldehyde Solution 37% (CAS # 50-00-0) and Monoisopropylamine (CAS #75-31-0). \n\nFORMALDEHYDE 37% SOLUTION\nThe facility receives a tanker trailer of Formaldehyde Solution (4,900 gallons) an average of twice a month.  Ambit stores a maximum of 10,000 gallons of the solution in two bulk storage tanks, which are located in an outdoor diked storage tank farm. It also stores two or three back up totes (300 gallons each) of the solution. \n\nMONOISOPROPYLAMINE\nMIPA is received in steel drums and stored in a warehouse building for protection. Pallets of"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 1,
      "FacilityID": 1000122008,
      "SummaryText": "drums are moved by forklift to the production scale as needed to charge them to a reactor as part of a batch procedure. Drums are checked for emptiness by drip-drying them. Used empty drums are sent out for recycling. \n\nGENERAL ACCIDENTAL RELEASE PREVENTION PROGRAM/CHEMICAL-SPECIFIC PREVENTION STEPS\nThis manufacturing facility is in compliance with state and federal laws and programs concerning the prevention of chemical spills including SWPPP, EPA's Accidental Release Prevention Rule, and OSHA's Process Safety Management rules. In addition, operators are trained in the proper handling and monitoring of Formaldehyde Solution when it is used in a manufacturing process. All employees receive regular training in spill prevention and spill clean-up.\n\nFIVE YEAR ACCIDENT HISTORY\nAccording to the requirements of the EPA Risk Management Program, accidental releases that involved deaths, injuries, or significant property damage on the site, or known offsite deaths, injuries, evacuations, sheltering in place, property damage, or environmental damage have to be reported in the RMP submission. \nThere has been no accidental release related to Formaldehyde Solution in the past five years meeting the definitions provided in 40 CFR Section 68.42.\nMIPA was brought on the site in 2024, and there have been no accidental releases that qualify under the RMP definitions.\n\nEMERGENCY RESPONSE PROGRAM\nThe Emergency Response Program focuses on taking all actions necessary to reduce the threat of exposure to employees, surrounding neighbors, and to the environment.  These efforts include:\n--Regularly updating the local fire department and local emergency response contact with an updated list of chemicals stored on the site, an updated map of where the chemicals are stored, and a copy of any SDS's for new chemicals stored on site are made available to them through Tier II reporting (on E-Plan) or as needed.\n--Ambit also tries to take representatives of the local fire department on tour of the"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 2,
      "FacilityID": 1000122008,
      "SummaryText": "plant whenever practical and whenever requested by the local fire departments. This allows the department personnel to become familiar with the plant layout and ask questions about the plant and the chemicals stored here. It also allows Ambit to get feedback from the fire department on improvements it might make to make the plant site safer or more accessible to emergency personnel. In addition, the fire department also trains Ambit employees in the correct use of fire extinguishers on the plant site.\n--Ambit employees go through regular safety training and review of emergency policies and procedures. Personnel also go through drills including what to do when an employee is injured and when/how to evacuate the plant in an emergency.\n\nPLANNED CHANGES TO IMPROVE SAFETY\n--Ambit will continue to bring in outside trainers and consultants to educate employees and management on proper precautions and procedures related to personal safety and the safety of others and the environment.\n--Ambit has begun adding cameras and monitors throughout the plant that will help ensure the safety of employees and the environment."
    }
  ]
